    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Depression mood accounted for a significant proportion of the variance of global EAT scores as well as of dieting and bulimia scores. Feelings about appearance accounted for a significant amount of variance in dieting scores among athletes. Perfectionism dimensions were not relevant in predicting eating disorders symptoms in this sample."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "First, as with any study using self-reported measures, findings may be susceptible to selective or erroneous reporting. Self reported measures always carry risks, especially with athletes who might not be forthcoming in their answers for fear of being eliminated from the team if they appear to be eating disordered. Second, the EAT-26 alone does not yield a specific diagnosis of an eating disorder. However, the use of a self-administered questionnaire, which incorporates Diagnostic and statistical manual of mental disorders, as we used in our study, increases the advantages of using this questionnaire, which has been used in many epidemiological or screening studies, even if each subscale sould be interpreted independently. Finally, the number of participants was small."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Further research is needed to more firmly etablish the prevalence eating disorders in the male athletes and further, extend the line of inquiry examining relationships among measures of eating disorders, mood, and personality of athletes."
    },
